When transitioning from traditional lighting to flexible rubber LED strips (often called LED neon flex), the process is relatively straightforward, but the hardware requirements change slightly due to the material's heat-dissipating properties and flexibility.
​Here is the breakdown of how to make that switch effectively:
​1. Choose the Right Material
​Silicone vs. PVC: Silicone-based "rubber" housing is superior to PVC. It is more UV-resistant (it won't yellow in the sun), remains flexible in cold weather, and handles heat dissipation better for the LEDs inside.
​IP Rating: Since rubber strips are often used for outdoor or "restoration" style projects, look for IP67 or IP68 ratings if they will be exposed to moisture.
​2. Power and Voltage
​Most rubberized LED strips run on 12V or 24V DC.
​If you are switching from standard 120V household lights, you will need a transformer (LED Driver).
​Calculation: Ensure your power supply wattage is at least 20% higher than the total wattage of the strip to prevent overheating.
​3. Mounting and Adhesion
​Rubber doesn't always play nice with standard double-sided tape because of its texture and flexibility.
​Mounting Clips: The most secure way to install these is using transparent plastic or aluminum mounting clips.
​Silicone Adhesive: If you must glue them, use a dedicated silicone sealant. Standard "Super Glue" can become brittle and crack the rubber housing over time.
​4. Cutting and Capping
​Cut Points: Look for the black lines or copper pads visible through the casing. You can only cut at these specific intervals.
​End Caps: This is the most important step for safety. Once you cut a rubber strip, the internal circuitry is exposed. You must use silicone end caps and a dab of sealant to keep the strip "sealed" and prevent electrical shorts.

